WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.826
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.126 --> 00:00:09.547
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.847 --> 00:00:17.306
Unit eleven: Literature Reviews. Today you will review earlier research with the present perfect and reporting verbs.

4
00:00:17.606 --> 00:00:20.143
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.077
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:26.092
Research gap.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:31.498
Research gap means a topic or question that earlier studies have not answered.

8
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.803
The research gap concerns rural schools.

9
00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:38.522
Your turn. Say: Research gap.

10
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:42.391
Research gap.

11
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.732
Thematic organization.

12
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:50.317
Thematic organization means arranging a review by ideas rather than by date.

13
00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.961
Thematic organization grouped the studies by topic.

14
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.841
Your turn. Say: Thematic organization.

15
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.282
Thematic organization.

16
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:04.656
Synthesis of sources.

17
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:10.540
Synthesis of sources means combining ideas from many sources into one clear picture.

18
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:14.241
Synthesis of sources shows the overall pattern.

19
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.141
Your turn. Say: Synthesis of sources.

20
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.849
Synthesis of sources.

21
00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:24.898
Scope statement.

22
00:01:25.883 --> 00:01:29.984
Scope statement means a sentence that says what a review will and will not cover.

23
00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:33.883
The scope statement excludes studies before 2000.

24
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:36.996
Your turn. Say: Scope statement.

25
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.160
Scope statement.

26
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:43.394
Chronological review.

27
00:01:44.383 --> 00:01:48.833
Chronological review means a review that follows research in order of time.

28
00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:52.484
A chronological review shows how ideas developed.

29
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:56.235
Your turn. Say: Chronological review.

30
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.798
Chronological review.

31
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.143
Critical evaluation.

32
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.961
Critical evaluation means judging the strengths and weaknesses of research.

33
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.362
Critical evaluation exposed a weak sample.

34
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.334
Your turn. Say: Critical evaluation.

35
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.782
Critical evaluation.

36
00:02:20.517 --> 00:02:22.075
Existing scholarship.

37
00:02:23.083 --> 00:02:27.823
Existing scholarship means the research and writing that already exist on a topic.

38
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:31.368
Existing scholarship agrees on the main cause.

39
00:02:31.983 --> 00:02:35.402
Your turn. Say: Existing scholarship.

40
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:40.150
Existing scholarship.

41
00:02:40.917 --> 00:02:42.301
Research question.

42
00:02:43.317 --> 00:02:47.232
Research question means the question that a new study will try to answer.

43
00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:49.990
The research question grew from the gap.

44
00:02:50.583 --> 00:02:53.874
Your turn. Say: Research question.

45
00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:58.106
Research question.

46
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:02.516
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:10.263
Visal, a review says 'Several studies have examined sleep, but few have explored memory.' Why the present perfect?

48
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:22.515
Because the research connects to the present. It tells us what exists now. Use have / has + past participle for earlier research whose results still matter.

49
00:03:23.617 --> 00:03:25.117
And when we name a writer?

50
00:03:26.217 --> 00:03:41.045
Use a reporting verb: Smith (2020) argues that… or Lee and Tan have shown that… Choose the verb carefully: shows is strong, suggests is cautious, claims may signal doubt.

51
00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.975
When is the past simple used?

52
00:03:45.083 --> 00:03:58.173
For a finished event: Smith conducted the survey in 2019. Use the present perfect for the research record, the past simple for the specific study, and present tense for the ideas.

53
00:03:59.333 --> 00:04:01.077
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:01.917 --> 00:04:07.631
Grammar focus: Reviewing research: have plus past participle and reporting verbs.

55
00:04:07.931 --> 00:04:09.141
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.208
Several studies have examined the effect of sleep on memory.

57
00:04:14.117 --> 00:04:16.848
Few researchers have explored rural classrooms.

58
00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.713
Lee and Tan have shown that reading aloud helps learners.

59
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:31.480
Use have / has + past participle for earlier research that still matters, a reporting verb (shows, suggests, argues) for a named source, and past simple for one finished study.

60
00:04:32.217 --> 00:04:36.854
Be careful! Don't say: Researchers have studied it in 2020.

61
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.320
Say: Researchers studied it in 2020.

62
00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.015
Use the past simple with a finished time.

63
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.209
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.349
Mapping the Field.

65
00:04:50.649 --> 00:04:55.029
A literature review begins with a scope statement that says what it will and will not cover.

66
00:04:56.033 --> 00:05:02.085
Existing scholarship has examined the topic widely, but few researchers have studied rural classrooms.

67
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:10.185
Some reviews use thematic organization, while others prefer a chronological review that follows ideas through time.

68
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.950
Synthesis of sources means that the writer has combined many studies into one picture, not just listed them.

69
00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:23.439
Critical evaluation then exposes the research gap, and the gap leads to a new research question.

70
00:05:24.533 --> 00:05:29.011
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.302
Researchers blank the topic for many years.

72
00:05:32.602 --> 00:05:36.325
A, have studied. B, are study. C, has study.

73
00:05:39.867 --> 00:05:41.808
The answer is A: have studied.

74
00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.656
A topic that earlier studies have not answered is a research blank.

75
00:05:46.956 --> 00:05:50.087
A, scope. B, gap. C, theme.

76
00:05:53.633 --> 00:05:55.180
The answer is B: gap.

77
00:05:56.317 --> 00:05:57.980
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:06.240
A, Few scholars has explored this area.. B, Few scholars have explored this area.. C, Few scholars have explore this area..

79
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.471
The answer is B: Few scholars have explored this area..

80
00:06:14.617 --> 00:06:17.789
A review that follows research in order of time is blank.

81
00:06:18.089 --> 00:06:22.009
A, thematic. B, critical. C, chronological.

82
00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:27.891
The answer is C: chronological.

83
00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.594
According to the reading, what leads to a new research question?

84
00:06:32.894 --> 00:06:37.546
A, the research gap. B, the title page. C, the scope only.

85
00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.970
The answer is A: the research gap.

86
00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:48.307
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:54.528
Why is identifying a 'gap' in existing research so important before starting a new study?

88
00:07:03.317 --> 00:07:04.840
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:30.530
Identifying a gap is important because it shows that the new study is needed. Without it, researchers may repeat work that has already been done. A clear gap also guides the research question and helps readers see the value of the project. Existing scholarship has already answered many questions, so a new study must add something that is truly missing.

90
00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.690
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:33.583 --> 00:07:40.415
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reviewing research: have plus past participle and reporting verbs.

92
00:07:40.715 --> 00:07:46.758
Today's value is intellectual curiosity. Ask deep questions, and keep exploring ideas.

93
00:07:47.058 --> 00:07:49.571
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:07:49.871 --> 00:07:53.080
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

